{
  "_about": "Controlled vocabulary for fingerprint cards. Every enumerated field on a card must use a key from here, so two builds that made the same choice collide on the same string. Sources: guide-to-prompt/references/domains and shapes, stumping Parts 1, 2, 6.1 and 6.2, determinism-check Gate G, guide-to-prompt SKILL.md section 3. Add a value only when no existing one fits, and add it here before using it on a card. stump_strategies is the family of the decisive move independent of the gap and pattern labels, and decisive_rule_filed plus official_decoy on a card mark the withheld-rule-with-official-decoy architecture whatever family label it carries; validated_decoy marks the build whose wrong method reproduces every closed, published or certified figure in the pack and breaks only on a forward condition. client_domains is the client's taxonomy (seven groups, twenty-one domains, each with its task types and the Axis 1 objectives each task type serves); house_domains are the nine domains the earlier spec named, still valid for cards and mapped to the client domains they sit in. layer2_families are the five families of the client's two-layer design; a card's layer2_family takes one key and its layer2_pattern names the pattern from stumping/references/client/layer2-rigor-patterns.md as <domain>/<task-type>/<n>, or 'own:<short name>' for a pattern built beyond the library. stump_classes classifies the decisive step under the client's three rules: only 'analytical' may decide the answer; a planted defect, decoy or hidden rule, a data-semantics reading and a stated-rule application can appear in a task but never as the decisive stump. A card drawn on or after 2026-10-10 also carries analyst_test (the easy route a strong analyst takes after cleaning the data and reading every rule correctly, and the wrong answer it gives) and domain_test (the field's own analysis that decides the answer, and why swapping the nouns into another field breaks the task).",
